question:
The figure below shows a grid of $10$ squares in a row. Each square has a diagonal connecting its lower left vertex to its upper right vertex. A bug moves along the line segments from vertex to vertex, never traversing the same segment twice and never moving from right to left along a horizontal or diagonal segment. Let $N$ be the number of paths the bug can take from the lower left corner ($A$) to the upper right corner ($B$). One such path from $A$ to $B$ is shown by the thick line segments in the figure. Find $\sqrt{N}$.

[asy]
size(12cm);
draw((0, 0)--(0, 1));
for (int i = 0; i <= 9; i += 1)
{
draw((i, 1)--(i + 1, 1)--(i + 1, 0)--(i, 0)--(i + 1, 1));
}
draw((0, 0)--(0, 1)--(2, 1)--(2, 0)--(3, 1)--(3, 0)--(4, 1)--(4, 0)--(5, 0)--(5, 1)--(6, 1)--(6, 0)--(7, 1)--(8, 1)--(8, 0)--(10, 0)--(10, 1), red + linewidth(2));
label("$A$", (0, 0), dir(180));
label("$B$", (10, 1), dir(0));
[/asy]

dapo_q:
How many paths are there from $A$ to $B$ on the lines of the grid shown, if every step must be up or to the right?[asy]size(3cm,3cm);int w=6;int h=3;int i;for (i=0; i<h; ++i){ draw((0,i) -- (w-1,i));}for (i=0; i<w; ++i){ draw((i, 0)--(i,h-1));}label("$B$", (w-1,h-1), NE);label("$A$", (0,0), SW);[/asy]
For example, here is one such valid path highlighted in blue:
[asy]
size(3cm,3cm);
int w=6;
int h=3;
int i;
for (i=0; i<h; ++i){ 
draw((0,i) -- (w-1,i));
}for (i=0; i<w; ++i){ 
draw((i, 0)--(i,h-1));
}
label("$B$", (w-1,h-1), NE);
label("$A$", (0,0), SW);

draw((0,0)--(1,0), blue+1.5);
draw((1,0)--(1,1), blue+1.5);
draw((1,1)--(2,1), blue+1.5);
draw((2,1)--(3,1), blue+1.5);
draw((3,1)--(4,1), blue+1.5);
draw((4,1)--(4,2), blue+1.5);
draw((4,2)--(5,2), blue+1.5);
[/asy]
